I’ll anchor the rule to documented/default-profile observables, then separate high-confidence protocol traits from customization-sensitive strings.
The default profile is most reliably identified by outbound `POST /submit.php?id=<numeric beacon ID>` requests carrying binary output:

```suricata
alert http $HOME_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET $HTTP_PORTS (
    msg:"MALWARE-C2 possible Cobalt Strike default Beacon HTTP POST";
    flow:established,to_server;
    http.method; content:"POST";
    http.uri; content:"/submit.php?id="; startswith; fast_pattern;
    pcre:"/^\/submit\.php\?id=[0-9]+$/";
    http.request_header; content:"application/octet-stream"; nocase;
    classtype:trojan-activity;
    sid:1000001;
    rev:1;
)
```

An additional, lower-confidence rule detects default check-ins using one of the stock URI paths and base64 metadata in the `Cookie` header:

```suricata
alert http $HOME_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET $HTTP_PORTS (
    msg:"MALWARE-C2 possible Cobalt Strike default Beacon HTTP GET";
    flow:established,to_server;
    http.method; content:"GET";
    http.uri;
    pcre:"/^\/(?:ca|dpixel|__utm\.gif|pixel\.gif|g\.pixel|dot\.gif|updates\.rss|fwlink|cm|cx|pixel|match|visit\.js|load|push|ptj|j\.ad|ga\.js|en_US\/all\.js|activity|IE9CompatViewList\.xml)$/";
    http.cookie;
    pcre:"/^[A-Za-z0-9+\/]{160,220}={0,2}$/";
    classtype:trojan-activity;
    sid:1000002;
    rev:1;
)
```

**Brittleness**
- **VERIFIED, high confidence:** `/submit.php`, the `id` parameter, stock GET URI pool, HTTP methods, `Cookie` placement, base64 transform, and `application/octet-stream` are all controlled by the Malleable C2 profile. An operator can change each without modifying Beacon itself.
- The cookie regex assumes the default base64-encoded metadata shape. Operators can use `mask`, `netbios`, `base64url`, prepend/append data, or move metadata into a URI parameter, another header, or the path.
- User-Agent signatures are especially brittle: the default already varies among Internet Explorer-style values, and profiles can override it.
- Sleep interval and periodicity are weak alone. Operators can change `sleeptime`, add jitter, or introduce traffic shaping.
- TLS hides all these HTTP elements unless Suricata receives decrypted traffic.
- `$EXTERNAL_NET` will miss callbacks to internal red-team redirectors if those addresses are part of `$HOME_NET`.

Treat the POST rule as relatively high-signal and the GET rule as a hunting alert because several stock paths are common in legitimate web traffic. Validate both against your Suricata version with `suricata -T -c /path/to/suricata.yaml -S /path/to/rules`.

